Here is a simple example of a red heart fractal in p5.js and WebGL:

```javascript
let angle = 0;
let scale = 0.9;

function setup() {
  createCanvas(600, 600, WEBGL);
}

function draw() {
  background(220);

  // translate and rotate to center the heart
  translate(0, 0, -200);
  rotateX(radians(angle));
  rotateY(radians(angle));

  // draw the heart
  drawHeart(0, 0, 0, 100, 20, 10);

  // increment the angle for rotation
  angle += 1;
}

// recursive function to draw the heart
function drawHeart(x, y, z, radius, width, depth) {
  if (depth > 0) {
    // top half
    fill(255, 0, 0); // red
    beginShape(TRIANGLES);
    vertex(x + radius, y + radius, z);
    vertex(x, y, z);
    vertex(x + radius, y - radius, z);
    endShape();

    // bottom half
    fill(255, 0, 0); // red
    beginShape(TRIANGLES);
    vertex(x + radius, y - radius, z);
    vertex(x, y, z);
    vertex(x + radius, y + radius, z);
    endShape();

    // recursive call for top part
    drawHeart(x, y + radius/2, z, radius * scale, width * scale, depth - 1);

    // recursive call for bottom part
    drawHeart(x, y - radius/2, z, radius * scale, width * scale, depth - 1);
  }
}
```

This code creates a 3D heart fractal by recursively drawing two halves of a heart shape and then attaching them at their shared edge. The fractal is rotated by changing the angle in each frame, creating a spinning effect. The fractal is also scaled down slightly each time to create the recursive pattern.<|start_header_id|>assistant<|end_header_id|>

