In Android, JVMTI (Java Virtual Machine Tool Interface) is a part of the Android Runtime (ART) that allows you to attach agents to the Android runtime and manipulate its behavior. One of the features of JVMTI is the ability to transform classes, which is also known as "class transformation" or "class modification".

Here are some code examples of JVMTI class transformations in Android:

**Example 1: Add a method to a class**
```java
public class MyClassTransformer implements ClassTransformer {
    @Override
    public byte[] transform(ClassFileServiceProvider provider, ClassFile file) {
        // Get the class file buffer
        DataBuffer buffer = file.getBuffer();

        // Find the method to add
        MethodInfo method = null;
        for (MethodInfo mi : file.getMethods()) {
            if (mi.getName().equals("originalMethod")) {
                method = mi;
                break;
            }
        }

        // Add a new method
        MethodInfo newMethod = new MethodInfo();
        newMethod.setName("newMethod");
        newMethod.setModifiers(MethodInfoModifiers.AccPublic);
        newMethod.setReturnType(type("int"));
        newMethod.setRetVal(0);

        // Create a new bytes arrays for the new method
        byte[] newMethodInfo = new byte[24]; // size of MethodInfo struct
        ByteArrayOutputStream bos = new ByteArrayOutputStream();
        bos.write(newMethodInfo);
        bos.write Uint16(0x1234); // methodId
        bos.write Uint16(0x5678); // accessFlags
        bos.write Uint16(0x0000); // exceptionTableLength
        bos.write Uint16(0x0000); // exceptionTable

        // Add the new method to the class file
        file.addMethod(newMethod);

        // Return the updated class file buffer
        return buffer.getData();
    }
}
```
